//? Title: Slerp: actual quaternion arc and branch
//? Subtitle: Actual canonical histories, retained original fixtures and explicit controls
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
Two orientations must be joined by a smooth rotation without leaving the unit
quaternion sphere. Spherical interpolation traverses the shorter great-circle
arc, with a near-parallel numerical fallback. We inspect the actual endpoint
normalization, sign choice and weights, and retain the original
identity-to90-degree rotation.
)

\toc

> Mechanism and mathematical scope

Unit quaternions represent rotations with a double cover: q and -q are the same
orientation. Normalize both endpoints, flip the target when their dot is
negative, and let Omega be the quaternion-space angle. The full physical
rotation angle is twice Omega.

$$ q(t)=\frac{\sin((1-t)\Omega)}{\sin\Omega}q_0+\frac{\sin(t\Omega)}{\sin\Omega}q_1.$$
\alttext(Spherical interpolation uses sine weights of the endpoint angle to move along the unit great circle.)

Decompose the target as cos(Omega) q0+sin(Omega) u with u orthogonal to q0.
Substituting the weights gives cos(t Omega) q0+sin(t Omega) u, proving unit norm
and constant angular rate in the spherical branch. The implementation normalizes
the computed result to remove small drift. When dot>0.9995, normalized chord
interpolation avoids a small sine denominator; this fallback is an approximation
and is not exactly constant-rate.

Normalization scales by the largest component before taking the norm, avoiding
overflow or underflow for finite nonzero raw representatives. Zero or nonfinite
quaternions are rejected. At t=1 the chosen representative may be -q1; the
orientation still matches. For exactly180-degree physical separation, either
shortest arc is valid and the supplied representative resolves the choice.

> A tiny actual producing history

The table and native concept diagram read the same producing execution. Every
block below is a complete editable C++ program.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/slerp_tiny.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
#include <filesystem>
#include <print>
#include <algorithm>
#include <cmath>
#include <limits>
#include <numeric>
#include <numbers>
#include <random>
#include <string>
#include <vector>
#include <map>
#include <set>
#include <array>
#include <tuple>
#include <bit>
#include <datamunge/random/quality.hpp>
#include <datamunge/algorithms/slerp.hpp>
int main() {
    namespace a = dm::algorithms;
    namespace g = dm::graphics;
    std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
    auto r =
        a::slerp_trace({1, 0, 0, 0}, a::quat_z_rotation(std::numbers::pi / 2),
                       .5);
    std::print("| actual normalized start | chosen target | result | dot | first weight | second weight | " "linear branch |\n| --- | --- | --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | --- |\n| `{}` | `{}` | `{}` | {} " "| {} | {} | {} |\n",
               r.start, r.target, r.value, r.dot, r.first_weight,
               r.second_weight, r.normalized_linear);
    g::Diagram concept_diagram(48, 1100);
    concept_diagram.node("n0", {0, 0}, "Identity to90-degree z rotation");
    concept_diagram.node("n1", {5, 0},
                         std::format("Spherical branch: {}",
                                     !r.normalized_linear));
    concept_diagram.node("n2", {10, 0},
                         std::format("Halfway angle {:.1f} degrees",
                                     a::quat_angle_between(r.start, r.value) *
                                     180 / std::numbers::pi));
    concept_diagram.edge("n0", "n1").edge("n1", "n2");
    concept_diagram.save_svg("build/slerp_tiny.svg");
}
```
