> API and interpretation

All fractions must be finite and in[0,1]; extrapolation is not part of this
checked API. Raw dot and norm helpers explicitly reject nonrepresentable
results, while normalization can still handle finite huge inputs by scaling
first. Run and trace return the same constant-size actual state. These are
controlled rotations, with no animation timing or pose dataset claim.

Reusable producers, fixtures and histories live in the canonical C++ library.
Captured records own their values and do not come from a separate report replay.
Selected counters and record/scalar policies are specified at their API
boundaries; they are not peak-byte or wall-time budgets. Synthetic structural
inputs are controlled fixtures for these mechanisms.

> References

- [Shoemake (1985), Animating rotation with quaternion curves|https://doi.org/10.1145/325334.325242]

